WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
ASP.NET Core can emit response headers from several places.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.320
Middleware can add headers across broad sections of the pipeline.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.980
Endpoint conventions or filters can apply behavior to selected routes.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.360
Static file configuration can affect asset responses.

5
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.020
Custom middleware can handle special cases.

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:27.800
Maintained security header packages may provide centralized policy support when their behavior is understood and reviewed.

7
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.720
Headers may also be added or modified outside the application.

8
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:43.380
Reverse proxies, ingress controllers, load balancers, CDNs, hosting platforms, and web servers can all change the final response seen by the browser.

9
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.880
That layered reality is common in production.

10
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.260
It also means header ownership must be clear.

11
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:56.880
Developers should know whether the app, proxy, CDN, or platform owns each policy.

12
00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.080
Different response paths may not behave the same way unless reviewed.

13
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:16.060
MVC views, Razor Pages, API responses, static assets, authentication pages, error pages, health endpoints, admin surfaces, and file downloads can follow different paths through the application and infrastructure.

14
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.280
A header visible on the home page may not be present on an error response or static file.

15
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.160
Duplicate or contradictory headers create confusing behavior.

16
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:33.180
If the application emits one policy and the CDN appends another, the browser may enforce a result the team did not intend.

17
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.760
If staging and production use different infrastructure rules, testing may not represent real behavior.

18
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:45.540
Header review should look at the final response as delivered, not only application code.

19
00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:49.420
Development settings should not silently become production policy.

20
00:01:49.520 --> 00:02:02.480
Local hosts, relaxed CSP settings, temporary framing permissions, verbose diagnostics, or preview deployment exceptions may be useful during implementation, but they should have clear environment scope.

21
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.220
Production policy should be explicit and owned.

22
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:14.940
The practical review question is simple: for each important response path, which layer emits the header, what value does the browser actually receive, and who owns changes.

23
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.240
Without that clarity, header configuration becomes hard to maintain and hard to trust.
